19047	{"artifactId":"LLD-c32aa60534232f36-s4","entry":{"id":"review-e72794bdd24fab8ee1a6bd9546db5db5","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-29T08:16:16.481Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD's acceptance-mapping table (ac1-ac5) covers exactly the five DEF acceptance criteria for s4 (crash recovery, uncertain attempt, concurrency fence, compatibility/allowances, usage replay) and no others.","evidence":"DEF.md §6.4 (STORY-c32aa60534232f36-s4) lists ac1 'accepted evidence...restored...not dispatched again; legacy runs...non-resumable', ac2 'outcome is marked uncertain', ac3 'only the current owner can advance...terminal publication cannot be duplicated', ac4 'reuse is explicitly validated...incompatible work is invalidated...inactive elapsed time and remaining execution allowance follow a declared policy', ac5 'attempt provenance survives...accepted replay is not counted as a new model call'. The LLD §7 'Acceptance mapping' table maps ac1-ac5 one-to-one to equivalent planned tests with matching language (crash-after-fsync replay, uncertain identity retention, generation fencing, compatibility/allowance accounting, replay-not-recharged usage). [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/DEF.md]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design builds nothing from the Epic's non-goals (no token-usage warehouse, no pricing/model-catalog change).","evidence":"DEF.md §3 non-goals: 'Introduce a new token-usage warehouse, model catalog or client-specific delivery-board UI.' LLD §3.5 explicitly states 'No usage warehouse/schema change' and routes through the existing writer via ReviewUsageObservationV1/recordAttemptUsage, which already exists unchanged in src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts. [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/DEF.md, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design's claim that durable append/idempotent storage and generation fencing are explicitly NOT yet implemented in the current BlockProgressCoordinator — i.e. that progress.ts today only produces an in-memory owner view and delegates real durability to sc4/sc6 — is true of the code as it stands.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts:9 doc comment: 'One owner view only. Durable append, idempotent storage and generation fencing are sc4/sc6 responsibilities.' The accept() method (lines 33-76) calls this.context.ports.stageImmutableValue/appendTransition — abstract ports with no durable implementation shipped in this file — and only mutates in-memory `this.current` via publish(); there is no persistence to disk anywhere in this file. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design's claim that the existing artifact-core write journal (sc2 of a different, unrelated epic) is a distinct mechanism from the new review-recovery runtime root, and that this separation is already architecturally established in the codebase, is true.","evidence":"src/workflow/artifact-core/journal.ts:6-13 doc comment: 'The write journal port... s2 defines the port and an in-memory implementation; s4 adds the durable one, a pending-head file per artifact under `.insrc/journal/`... s4 does not acquire a forbidden backwards dependency' — this journal recovers staged ARTIFACT heads (DEF/HLD/LLD/PLAN/CR), unrelated to investigation runtime state, matching the LLD's invariant '[[c2]] Keep runtime recovery separate from canonical artifact staged-head journal and lifecycle.' [files: src/workflow/artifact-core/journal.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"ReviewRuntimePortsV1 / ReviewUsageObservationV1 / ReviewReadReceiptV1, which the LLD treats as an EXISTING, unchanged foundational contract that s4 merely implements, already exist with exactly the shape the LLD quotes.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts:19-39 defines ReviewRuntimePortsV1 (readEvidence/stageImmutableValue/appendTransition/recordAttemptUsage), ReviewUsageObservationV1 and ReviewReadReceiptV1 verbatim-matching the LLD §2.3 and §3.5 interface text; continuation-types.ts:4 imports these types rather than redefining them, confirming they are pre-existing (s1-owned, per HLD §5.6 sc6) and not newly introduced by this Story. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation-types.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No durable implementation of these ports, and no on-disk review-recovery runtime store, already exists elsewhere in the code-review module — so labeling ReviewRecoveryV1 / the journal / the durable store as 'new' (§3.1-3.5) is accurate and not a duplicate of existing work.","evidence":"A search of src/workflow/code-review for 'review-investigation' / 'runtime root' finds no matches; the only existing persistence under `.insrc/review-runs/` is a metadata-only diagnostics file (src/daemon/__tests__/workflow-review-rpc.test.ts:244, src/workflow/review-pipeline/__tests__/review-cycle2.test.ts:40, both writing a flat `.jsonl` event log, not a reconstructible journal/checkpoint store), matching the HLD's own distinction: 'Existing metadata-only .insrc/review-runs/<runId>.jsonl diagnostics remain content-free and are not recovery checkpoints' (HLD.md:44). [files: src/daemon/__tests__/workflow-review-rpc.test.ts, src/workflow/review-pipeline/__tests__/review-cycle2.test.ts, docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/HLD.md]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The LLD names every new source file that will implement the sc4 runtime (the ReviewRecoveryV1 store, the journal, the attempt-reservation wrapper, the usage outbox), so a builder knows where each new component lives.","evidence":"Every 'Call sites' entry in §3 (3.1-3.5) and every contract in §2 names only pre-existing files already owned by s3 — progress.ts, continuation-types.ts, types.ts — as consumers/call sites; no 'Call sites' or any other field anywhere in the document names a new module (e.g. a recovery-store.ts, journal.ts, attempt-ledger.ts or usage-outbox.ts under src/workflow/code-review/investigation/) that will actually hold the new durable store, journal-append logic, lease/generation fencing or the usage-outbox implementation described in §3.1-3.5. The HLD explicitly expects the LLD to pick these: 'concrete filenames are selected in LLD, not claimed as existing implementations' (HLD.md §3). [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/HLD.md]","action":"Add explicit new-file paths (one per major new component: recovery store, journal/event log, attempt reservation wrapper, usage outbox) to the Data model / Call sites sections before build so the implementer and reviewer can trace each contract to a concrete home.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The error codes the LLD attributes to ReviewRuntimePortError (invalid identity/value, stale generation, sequence conflict, unknown reference) exactly match the existing exported error class's code union, so the error-path contract in §2.3/§6 is grounded in the real type rather than invented.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts:81-84 defines `export class ReviewRuntimePortError extends Error { constructor(readonly code: 'invalid-value' | 'invalid-identity' | 'stale-generation' | 'sequence-conflict' | 'unknown-reference', ...) }`, matching the LLD §2.3 'Errors: ReviewRuntimePortError when Invalid identity/value, stale generation, sequence conflict or unknown reference' one-for-one. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/types.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The runtime storage location the LLD specifies for the new ReviewRecoveryV1 store is consistent with the location the approved upstream HLD proposed for this exact mechanism.","evidence":"HLD.md line 44 ('PERSISTENCE AND RELATIONSHIPS'): 'Proposed storage is <project>/.insrc/review-runs/investigation-v1/<runId>/ with append-only transition records... generated runtime state must be excluded from Git attribution, indexing and review manifests', and HLD §8 Rollout D backward-compat: 'Runtime namespace is excluded from Git changes and indexing' — both describing a PROJECT-RELATIVE path inside the repo (consistent with the existing project-relative `.insrc/review-runs/` and `.insrc/journal/` conventions seen in src/workflow/artifact-core/journal.ts:23 and the review-cycle2/workflow-review-rpc tests). The LLD §3.1 instead places the store at '~/.insrc/review-investigation/<sha256(repoId)>/<validated runId>/' — the per-user HOME directory (src/shared/paths.ts:4 confirms `~/.insrc` is the daemon's global, cross-repo state root, e.g. config.json, graph.lmdb, lance/), a different location with different ownership/cleanup/multi-repo semantics (the sha256(repoId) segment is only needed because the store is no longer project-scoped) than what the approved HLD specified, and the LLD gives no rationale for this deviation. [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/HLD.md, src/shared/paths.ts, src/workflow/artifact-core/journal.ts]","action":"Reconcile the storage location with the approved HLD (either justify and record the deviation from the project-relative path to the home-directory path, including its effect on the HLD's git/indexing-exclusion backward-compat claim, or align the LLD with the HLD's project-relative path) before build.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The usage-outbox design (persist pending observation before calling the writer, replace with a receipt only on durable success) matches how the existing s3 code already calls the usage port, so no incompatible double-write/double-charge is introduced.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation-usage.ts:26-41 `recordInvestigationUsage` already marks the observation 'pending' via `context.onState(pending)' BEFORE calling `context.ports.recordAttemptUsage(observation)`, and only promotes to the port's returned state afterward — exactly the sequencing the LLD §3.5 describes ('Persist pending event before calling existing usage writer... Durable receipt event marks recorded; writer failure remains pending'). [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation-usage.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD's 'Adjacent scope' notes for s1/s2/s3/s5/s6 are verbatim consistent with the approved HLD's Story-boundary section, so s4 has not silently redefined another Story's ownership.","evidence":"LLD §1 adjacent-scope bullets for s1, s2, s3, s5, s6 reproduce the HLD §6.1-6.3, 6.5, 6.6 boundary text almost verbatim (e.g. s3's 'In-memory ports are test scaffolding while production selection remains disabled until s4/s6; no durable storage implementation or terminal approval logic' matches HLD.md §6.3 word-for-word), confirming s4's design was authored against, not in substitution for, the approved sibling boundaries. [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/HLD.md]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.2","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD's §3.3 change to BlockProgressTransitionV1 validation re-uses s2's existing ledger-delta recomputation (applyReviewLedgerDelta) rather than redefining sc2's coverage/evidence semantics.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts:2,53 imports and calls `applyReviewLedgerDelta` from ledger.ts (the s2-owned module) to compute the candidate ledger inside accept(); the LLD's Interaction table (§5) states for sc2: 'Replay and validate ledger deltas/provenance with existing sc2 helpers; do not change criteria or findings semantics' — matching the existing reuse pattern rather than introducing a parallel coverage engine. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/ledger.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The 'Crash during blob staging or journal append' error path's claim that accept() only ACKs after a successful append, and marks the state uncertain rather than failed on an append-outcome exception, matches the real control flow in progress.ts.","evidence":"progress.ts:65-75: `appendStarted=true` is set only right before calling `appendTransition`; the `catch` block does `if(appendStarted)this.publish({...this.current,uncertainProgressId:p.progressId})` and always throws an InvestigationError with code 'acceptance-conflict' and message distinguishing 'Transition acceptance outcome is uncertain; no ACK' from 'Transition staging failed; no ACK' — matching the LLD's error-path row 'No ACK on failed append... Provider interrupted after possible execution... Mark uncertain, retain accepted blocks'. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The 'Concurrent resume or late worker' error path's claim that every owner-bound mutation re-checks the generation/lease via assertOwner immediately before each committal step is true of the current continuation code, giving the durable fencing something real to extend.","evidence":"continuation.ts:36-67 calls `context.assertOwner(turn.generation)` (wrapped as `check()`) before dispatch, before/after staging in progress.ts (lines 36,58,65,67), and again in the `finally` block before accounting — i.e. ownership is re-asserted at every durable-boundary point, consistent with the LLD's 'Serialized owner-bound mutation checks leaseId, persisted generation, expiry and daemon process guard immediately before commit.' [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/progress.ts]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every s4 DEF acceptance criterion (ac1-ac5) is mapped to at least one named planned test in the LLD's acceptance-mapping table, with no criterion left without a test.","evidence":"LLD §7 'Acceptance mapping' table has one row per ac1 through ac5, each listing one or more concrete planned tests (e.g. ac1 → 'Crash after block fsync before ACK and after ACK...', 'Accept two blocks inside one provider request then kill worker...'; ac5 → 'Identical replay does not increment attempt/model usage...', 'Crash after usage writer success before receipt resends stable key once...'), and these same tests are also listed under the unit/integration/contract 'Subjects' breakdown earlier in §7, so the mapping is internally consistent rather than a bare restatement of the criteria. [files: docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/S004/LLD.md]","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.2","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The test strategy's integration fixtures (child-process crash harness, two-client-of-one-daemon-store, deterministic two-block backend) are plausible to build against the existing test scaffolding for this module, not invented fixtures with no realistic home.","evidence":"The investigation module's existing __tests__ directory (continuation-integration.test.ts, progress.test.ts, continuation-fixtures.ts, ledger-integration.test.ts) shows a precedent for integration-style fixtures over the real BlockProgressCoordinator/runInvestigationContinuation, but none of these existing files contain a child-process crash harness or a two-client-of-one-daemon-store fixture (those are new fixtures this Story would have to build, since the durable store itself does not exist yet), so I cannot confirm from current code that the described crash-harness fixture design is buildable as specified versus merely plausible. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/__tests__/continuation-integration.test.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/__tests__/progress.test.ts]","action":"Treat the crash-harness and multi-client fixtures as new test infrastructure to be built in this Story; verify their feasibility (e.g. can a test actually fork/kill a worker process against the chosen storage root) during build rather than assuming the LLD's fixture sketch is already achievable.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":2,"low":14,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-29T08:16:16.481Z","model":"cli-claude:claude-sonnet-5","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
